#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b85d035c-ce66-5d55-8159-c3de5b3fe4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85d035c-ce66-5d55-8159-c3de5b3fe4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257009} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a9c9fc-6d99-5e9c-a617-fc21b7aa4cce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ef22d3-07d6-525f-b1e0-653bfae49fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113886" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9282bab5-deb0-5c96-8746-c1502da05608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9282bab5-deb0-5c96-8746-c1502da05608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25d2f28-6227-5300-8084-fcf842ad6d18" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1804c8ea-5802-59e5-92d9-206715562863" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbded5ce-14f9-54a9-9b89-593da772eac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbded5ce-14f9-54a9-9b89-593da772eac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257024} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4bf4bc-7f65-51e5-a960-8db46f7bb8d9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23c94ac6-51bf-5545-8513-402f0a143fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113941" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "777ada17-74df-558c-8eb7-5eba2e110bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "777ada17-74df-558c-8eb7-5eba2e110bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260602} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a4bf30-e9a1-5c7c-9c08-9eae7b2d0bcf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae0c14e-2353-57d2-951a-f4c503f5887d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49189" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8642350-2991-5893-83fc-a1e23a9a6de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8642350-2991-5893-83fc-a1e23a9a6de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261478} :answer-field/id #uuid "94bbf047-d851-5069-9d13-32de162deb7d" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59c11097-0528-5954-9433-d9c9f67d6be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59c11097-0528-5954-9433-d9c9f67d6be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261480} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed86a1f-eab0-54d3-89ce-90c324fae487" :answer-field/key "f-of-x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc6ae514-8e76-5ad4-a9b5-b333e0e65f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$\n\nF of x: {{f-of-x}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}]}
